\f0\fs26 \cf0 Anger **Staff** \'97 The impulse to strike, to assert force. A staff raised is threat before action. Fits anger's character as impulse exceeding due measure.\
\
Erotic Passion **Grapes** \'97 Ancient symbol of intoxication, Dionysian excess, desire that overwhelms reason. Universally understood in the ancient world.\
\
Longing **Figs** \'97 Figs dry and preserve \'97 they are the *memory* of summer fruit. Pothos is longing for what was or what is absent. A dried fig is past sweetness.\
\
Love of Pleasure **Olives / Olive oil** \'97 The richest everyday pleasure in the ancient Mediterranean. Not luxury, but the pleasure that saturates ordinary life. Fits philedonia's character as diffuse pleasure-seeking.\
\
Love of Wealth) **Silver owl coin** \'97 You already have this in your list. A coin is the most direct symbol of wealth-love. The owl coin specifically connects to Athens, commerce, and the value placed on accumulation.\
\
Love of Honour **Limestone fragment with painted traces** \'97 The remnant of public architecture \'97 something built to be seen and admired, now faded. Philodoxia is the passion for reputation that outlasts the self. A fragment of painted public stone is exactly right.\
\
Enchantment / being charmed **Wax tablets** \'97 The tablet receives any impression pressed into it. Kelesis is being captured by a pleasing impression without examination. The wax that takes any shape is the mind that assents too easily.\
\
Malicious Joy **Pottery (cracked or broken)** \'97 Joy at another's broken vessel. The pleasure taken in seeing something of another's fail or shatter.\
\
Excessive Amusement **Lentils or beans** \'97 The simplest, most ordinary food elevated to excessive enjoyment. Terpsis is the trivial pleasure that consumes disproportionate attention. There's something right about a bowl of lentils as the symbol of amusement that goes too far.\
\
Terror **Leather sandals** \'97 The sandal that is frozen mid-step. Terror is the body stopped by an impression of overwhelming danger. Sandals represent movement arrested.\
\
Timidity / Shrinking **Chit\uc0\u333 n (tunic)** \'97 The garment pulled close, the person who makes themselves small. Oknos is the shrinking from action. A plain tunic drawn inward fits.\
\
Shame **the pallium (cloak)** \'97 the garment pulled up to hide the face.\
\
Dread / Astonishment **Limestone fragment** \'97 The sudden confrontation with something vast and ancient. Thambos is the dread-astonishment at something that exceeds comprehension. If limestone is used for philodoxia, consider **grain** \'97 the overwhelming abundance that produces awe.\
\
Panic **Barley bread** \'97 Panic is the most ordinary fear made irrational. Barley bread is the most ordinary food. There's something fitting about the staple of daily life representing the passion that disrupts ordinary life most completely.\
\
Anguish / Striving anxiety **wax tablets** (the mind that cannot stop writing and rewriting anxious futures). \
\
Pity **Milk** \'97 Milk is nourishment given to the vulnerable. Pity is the false belief that another's misfortune is a genuine evil. Milk given in pity rather than rational care.\
\
Envy **cheese** \'97 the preserved, accumulated good that another has and you do not.\
\
Jealousy **fish** \'97 fish in ancient Athens were luxury items, objects of competitive social display.\
\
Grief **Pallium (cloak)** \'97 The mourning garment. The rough wool cloak is the garment of the philosopher but also of the grieving. \
\
Burden / Oppressive grief **Onions** \'97 Onions make you weep. There is something honest and slightly darkly comic about this \'97 achos is the weight that presses tears out. In the ancient world, onions were also associated with the labour of the poor, the burden of ordinary life.}